# One repository serves both Linux and Windows, so line endings are pinned
# rather than left to each contributor's core.autocrlf. A bash script checked in
# with CRLF fails at the shebang, which is a confusing way to break the Linux
# side from a Windows machine. PowerShell reads LF without complaint, so LF
# everywhere is the safe choice.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.png binary
*.gif binary
*.bin binary
*.wav binary

# Windows Script Host is happier with CRLF, and this file is Windows-only.
*.vbs text eol=crlf
